---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RomanNumeralConverter from '@/components/tools/everyday/RomanNumeralConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="roman-numeral-converter">
  <RomanNumeralConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the Roman numeral converter</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Number → Roman</strong>: type a whole number from 1 to 3,999,999. The result has the correct
        overline for values above 3,999, and an ASCII-safe version in parentheses that you can paste
        anywhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Roman → Number</strong>: type or paste a numeral. The converter reads overlines, macrons and
        the (MMM) parenthesis convention, and explains any non-standard spelling.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Both directions show a step table with the value of each symbol.
    </p>

    <h2>The seven symbols</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Symbol</th>
            <th scope="col">Value</th>
            <th scope="col">Origin of the letter</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>I</td><td>1</td><td>A single tally mark</td></tr>
          <tr><td>V</td><td>5</td><td>An open hand with five fingers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>X</td><td>10</td><td>Two hands, or a crossed tally</td></tr>
          <tr><td>L</td><td>50</td><td>An adapted Etruscan character</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C</td><td>100</td><td>Latin centum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D</td><td>500</td><td>Half of the old symbol for 1,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>M</td><td>1000</td><td>Latin mille</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The rules of standard notation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Add left to right. Symbols are written from largest to smallest and their values are added:
        MCLXVI = 1,000 + 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 + 1 = 1,166.
      </li>
      <li>
        Subtract when a smaller symbol comes first. Only six pairs are valid: IV (4),
        IX (9), XL (40), XC (90), CD (400) and CM (900). I can precede only V and X, X only L and C, C
        only D and M.
      </li>
      <li>
        I, X, C and M repeat at most three times. IIII becomes IV; XXXX becomes XL.
      </li>
      <li>
        V, L and D never repeat. VV would be X, so it is never written.
      </li>
      <li>
        There is no zero and there are no fractions. The Romans wrote fractions with a separate duodecimal
        system of dots and symbols, and used the word nulla for nothing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The converter applies all of these rules. If you type IIX, it tells you that the standard spelling is
      VIII.
    </p>

    <h2>Numbers above 3,999: the vinculum</h2>
    <p>
      With M as the largest symbol, plain notation stops at MMMCMXCIX (3,999). Above that, the Romans drew a
      bar (a vinculum) over a numeral to multiply it by 1,000:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Value</th>
            <th scope="col">With an overline</th>
            <th scope="col">ASCII form</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>4,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">IV</span></td><td>(IV)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">V</span></td><td>(V)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">X</span></td><td>(X)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">L</span></td><td>(L)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">C</span></td><td>(C)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000,000</td><td><span style="text-decoration: overline">M</span></td><td>(M)</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td>3,999,999</td>
            <td><span style="text-decoration: overline">MMMCMXCIX</span>CMXCIX</td>
            <td>(MMMCMXCIX)CMXCIX</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A bar is easily lost in plain text, email and code, so the same value is often written with
      parentheses. This converter writes and reads both forms. 3,999,999 is the practical limit of the
      single-bar system. A double bar for millions existed but was never standardised.
    </p>

    <h2>Years in Roman numerals</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Year</th>
            <th scope="col">Roman numeral</th>
            <th scope="col">Year</th>
            <th scope="col">Roman numeral</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1900</td><td>MCM</td><td>2000</td><td>MM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1969</td><td>MCMLXIX</td><td>2010</td><td>MMX</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1984</td><td>MCMLXXXIV</td><td>2020</td><td>MMXX</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1990</td><td>MCMXC</td><td>2024</td><td>MMXXIV</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1999</td><td>MCMXCIX</td><td>2025</td><td>MMXXV</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2001</td><td>MMI</td><td>2026</td><td>MMXXVI</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A year is converted as one whole number, so film copyright lines and building cornerstones read
      MCMLXXXIV, not one numeral per digit.
    </p>

    <h2>Where Roman numerals are still used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Clock and watch faces, where IIII for 4 is a design tradition and not correct notation</li>
      <li>Book front matter, chapter and act numbers, and legal outline levels</li>
      <li>Monarchs, popes and dynastic names, such as Elizabeth II and Louis XIV</li>
      <li>Super Bowls, Olympiads and other numbered events</li>
      <li>Film and television copyright dates, historically to make the year less obvious at a glance</li>
      <li>Building cornerstones, monuments and memorial inscriptions</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reading a numeral quickly</h2>
    <p>
      Work left to right and compare each symbol with the one after it. If it is greater than or equal
      to the next, add it; if it is smaller, subtract it. MCMXCIV: M (add 1,000), C before M (subtract
      100), M (add 1,000), X before C (subtract 10), C (add 100), I before V (subtract 1), V (add 5) →
      1,000 + 900 + 90 + 4 = 1,994.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion runs in your browser as you type.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
